{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact package/builder-room design draft6 at5346406f4068d84313e3cbca831887c90f37b838, treedf22ebe964180022998b30a9d37839470d479701.\n\nF1 (P1), primary lines164–184: the creation-failure exception is not a lifetime item/key bound. The assertion that an item ending through create.refused/conflict never relates is false. A row can open an item of a max1 type, send relate with that item, and also create a child. Its refused creation result can then be the only writer of the type's final state, freeing the live slot for the next opening. No child need ever send. Two such openings from the same sender produce two retained relation keys, though this rule would prove instance1 times key1. Requiring the create to be in the opening row does not prevent the opening row, or another row before its result, from relating that item.\n\nThe actual pure validators/judges/folds confirm the counterexample: one sender, related type live max1; opening items1 and3 each emits summary before its child genesis is refused by a valid false guard; authenticated native creation-result delivery takes each item into failed. Those refused/conflict clauses in the opening row are the only final-state writers. With receiver copies1, first relation applies and second is refused type-full; with copies2, both apply. No production guard/assertion was changed. Probe /private/tmp/checker-m2a-interface-2xdpvocs/repo/checker-package-failed-key.mts and report /private/tmp/checker-m2a-interface-2xdpvocs/package-failed-key-validation.json. These use actual pure definition validation, creation/delivery judgments and folds with TEST in-memory authority/scripted founding; they do not implement validatePackage, from.pin, native installation or a provider.\n\nSeparate the successful-child-instance argument from the related-item-key argument. A creation-failure row may bound successful child instances because refused children cannot emit effective sends; it does not bound 